### What is the Pacific Atlantic Water Flow problem?

You are given an m x n grid of cell heights. Water flows from a cell to any of its four neighbors with equal or lower height. The Pacific Ocean borders the top row and left column. The Atlantic Ocean borders the bottom row and right column. You must return all cells from which water can reach both oceans.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class PacificAtlanticFlow {
    private static final int[][] DIRS = {{0,1},{0,-1},{1,0},{-1,0}};

    public List<int[]> pacificAtlantic(int[][] heights) {
        int rows = heights.length, cols = heights[0].length;
        boolean[][] pacific = new boolean[rows][cols];
        boolean[][] atlantic = new boolean[rows][cols];

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            dfs(heights, pacific, r, 0);
            dfs(heights, atlantic, r, cols - 1);
        }
        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
            dfs(heights, pacific, 0, c);
            dfs(heights, atlantic, rows - 1, c);
        }

        List<int[]> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
                if (pacific[r][c] && atlantic[r][c])
                    result.add(new int[]{r, c});
        return result;
    }

    private void dfs(int[][] heights, boolean[][] visited, int r, int c) {
        visited[r][c] = true;
        for (int[] d : DIRS) {
            int nr = r + d[0], nc = c + d[1];
            if (nr >= 0 && nr < heights.length && nc >= 0 && nc < heights[0].length
                    && !visited[nr][nc] && heights[nr][nc] >= heights[r][c]) {
                dfs(heights, visited, nr, nc);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Why reverse the flow direction?

The naive approach tests every cell by running a BFS or DFS to see if water from that cell reaches both oceans. That is extremely expensive because you run a full search from each of the m x n cells.

Reversing the flow means starting from each ocean's border and climbing uphill. A cell reached by this reversed search can drain water downhill back to that ocean. This turns m x n separate searches into just two border-seeded sweeps, each visiting every cell at most once.

```java
// Naive: O((m*n)^2) - search from every cell
// Reversed: O(m*n) - two border-seeded sweeps

// The reversed DFS condition:
// if (heights[nr][nc] >= heights[r][c])  // climb uphill
// This models: water at [nr][nc] can flow DOWN to [r][c]
```

### How does the DFS work internally?

Each DFS starts from a border cell and explores all four neighbors. A neighbor is visited only if its height is greater than or equal to the current cell's height (climbing uphill) and it has not already been marked. Marking a cell means water from that cell can reach the ocean that seeded the search.

```java
// DFS from Pacific border cell (0, 0):
// heights = [[3, 3, 3],
//            [3, 1, 3],
//            [3, 3, 3]]
// Pacific seeds: top row (0,0),(0,1),(0,2) and left col (1,0),(2,0)
// From (0,0): height=3, neighbors (0,1)=3 >= 3 -> visit
// From (1,0): height=3, neighbor (1,1)=1 < 3 -> skip
// Center cell (1,1) is NOT reachable from Pacific border
```

### How do you use BFS instead of DFS?

BFS is an alternative to DFS for this problem. You seed a queue with all border cells for one ocean, then process the queue level by level, climbing uphill. The result is identical.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class PacificAtlanticBFS {
    private static final int[][] DIRS = {{0,1},{0,-1},{1,0},{-1,0}};

    public List<int[]> pacificAtlantic(int[][] heights) {
        int rows = heights.length, cols = heights[0].length;
        boolean[][] pacific = new boolean[rows][cols];
        boolean[][] atlantic = new boolean[rows][cols];

        Queue<int[]> pQueue = new LinkedList<>();
        Queue<int[]> aQueue = new LinkedList<>();

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++) {
            pQueue.offer(new int[]{r, 0}); pacific[r][0] = true;
            aQueue.offer(new int[]{r, cols-1}); atlantic[r][cols-1] = true;
        }
        for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++) {
            pQueue.offer(new int[]{0, c}); pacific[0][c] = true;
            aQueue.offer(new int[]{rows-1, c}); atlantic[rows-1][c] = true;
        }

        bfs(heights, pacific, pQueue);
        bfs(heights, atlantic, aQueue);

        List<int[]> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
                if (pacific[r][c] && atlantic[r][c])
                    result.add(new int[]{r, c});
        return result;
    }

    private void bfs(int[][] heights, boolean[][] visited, Queue<int[]> queue) {
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int[] cell = queue.poll();
            for (int[] d : DIRS) {
                int nr = cell[0] + d[0], nc = cell[1] + d[1];
                if (nr >= 0 && nr < heights.length && nc >= 0 && nc < heights[0].length
                        && !visited[nr][nc] && heights[nr][nc] >= heights[cell[0]][cell[1]]) {
                    visited[nr][nc] = true;
                    queue.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### How is DFS different from BFS here?

| Aspect | DFS | BFS |
|---|---|---|
| Implementation | Recursive or explicit stack | Queue-based |
| Space | O(m*n) for recursion stack in worst case | O(m*n) for queue in worst case |
| Order of exploration | Depth-first, follows one path deep | Level-by-level from border |
| Result | Identical reachability sets | Identical reachability sets |
| Risk | Stack overflow on very large grids | No stack overflow risk |

```java
// DFS: simpler code, risk of stack overflow on large grids
// BFS: slightly more code, safer for production with large grids
```

### What are the edge cases?

A single-cell grid always reaches both oceans because that cell is on all four borders. A grid where all heights are equal means every cell reaches both oceans. A grid that slopes strictly from top-left to bottom-right may have only the top-left corner reaching the Pacific and the bottom-right corner reaching the Atlantic.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class PacificAtlanticEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        PacificAtlanticFlow solver = new PacificAtlanticFlow();

        // Single cell: always reaches both
        int[][] single = {{5}};
        System.out.println(solver.pacificAtlantic(single).size()); // 1

        // All equal: every cell reaches both
        int[][] flat = {{3,3},{3,3}};
        System.out.println(solver.pacificAtlantic(flat).size()); // 4
    }
}
```

```text
