4
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to reverse the flow direction, comparing heights[nr][nc] <= heights[r][c] instead of >=. That would search downhill from the border, which marks cells the ocean can flood rather than cells that can drain to the ocean.

```java
// WRONG: searching downhill from border
if (heights[nr][nc] <= heights[r][c]) { ... }  // Bug!

// CORRECT: searching uphill from border (reversed flow)
if (heights[nr][nc] >= heights[r][c]) { ... }
```

Another mistake is not seeding all border cells. The Pacific border includes both the top row and the left column, and the Atlantic border includes both the bottom row and the right column.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should understand that the key insight is the reversal of direction. Searching outward from every cell to both oceans is O((m*n)^2), while seeding from the borders and climbing uphill is O(m*n). The equal-or-greater comparison correctly models the reversed downhill flow.

For very large grids, prefer BFS over recursive DFS to avoid stack overflow. The iterative DFS with an explicit stack is equally safe.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:
- Why reverse the flow? (Reduces m*n searches to two border-seeded sweeps.)
- What is the time complexity? (O(m*n) because each cell is visited at most twice.)
- DFS or BFS? (Both work; BFS avoids stack overflow on large grids.)
- What if water flows only to strictly lower neighbors? (Change >= to > in the reversed search.)
